"""
# Networking under a NAMED engine — executable probe

**Feature IDs:** #NET-001 to #NET-010
**Category:** Runtime | Networking
**Status:** Implemented (with one measured, still-OPEN engine gap — see below)

## Why this is a runnable program and not an sspec `describe`

`test/03_system/feature/usage/networking_spec.spl` carries a describe block
titled *"JIT Compilation Mode"* whose two examples were:

    it "tcp bind compiles in JIT mode":
        @net
        fn test_tcp_jit() -> bool:
            true            # <- the entire assertion
        expect test_tcp_jit()

That is doubly empty. It binds no socket, so it asserts nothing about
networking; and an sspec body physically cannot execute under the Cranelift
JIT anyway (`describe` / `it` / `expect` are Rust interpreter intrinsics with
no codegen lowering, and a spec file has no `fn main`, so the driver routes it
to `evaluate_module` regardless of `SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE` — see
`scripts/check/check-engine-claiming-specs-use-probe.shs`). It would have
stayed green with the whole network stack removed.

This program is the gate that block was claiming to be: it actually binds a
TCP listener and a UDP socket, closes them, and rejects a malformed address,
as a runnable program with `fn main`, and self-scores a verdict line.

Run directly, once per engine:

    SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpret <simple> run <this file>
    SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=jit       <simple> run <this file>

Score the `PROBE VERDICT:` LINE, never the exit code.

## The measured finding: TCP/UDP bind does NOT compile in JIT mode

The block's title is false as written. Measured 2026-08-09 on
`bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple`:

    $ SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=jit simple run <this file>
    [jit-fallback] unresolved external symbol 'native_tcp_bind': whole module
    dropped to the interpreter (expect ~100-1000x slowdown).

    $ SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT=1 SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=jit simple run <this file>
    error: Cranelift JIT compile: Module error: SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT: unresolved
    external symbol 'native_tcp_bind' would NULL-jump in JIT; refusing to fall
    back to the interpreter

`native_udp_bind` behaves identically on its own. So the socket externs are
NOT registered with the JIT module: the lane the old titles named is not
merely unasserted, it is unreachable, and every `SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=jit`
run of networking code is silently an interpreter run. Filed as
`doc/08_tracking/bug/jit_cannot_resolve_native_socket_externs_2026-08-09.md`.

Consequently the calling spec asserts the FALLBACK, not a JIT pass: it reads
the `[jit-fallback] unresolved external symbol 'native_tcp_bind'` line off
stderr. That assertion is a pin on measured current reality, not approval —
when the externs are registered with the JIT it must be updated to assert a
real compiled run instead of a fallback.

Bind uses port 0 (kernel-assigned) so the probe never collides with a
listener already on the box and needs no cleanup beyond its own close.
"""

extern fn native_tcp_bind(addr: text) -> (i64, i64)
extern fn native_tcp_close(handle: i64) -> i64
extern fn native_udp_bind(addr: text) -> (i64, i64)
extern fn native_udp_close(handle: i64) -> i64

var failures = 0

fn check_bool(name: text, got: bool, want: bool) -> void:
    if got == want:
        print("  ok   " + name)
    else:
        print("  FAIL " + name + " (got " + got.to_text() + ", want " + want.to_text() + ")")
        failures = failures + 1

fn check_i64(name: text, got: i64, want: i64) -> void:
    if got == want:
        print("  ok   " + name)
    else:
        print("  FAIL " + name + " (got " + got.to_text() + ", want " + want.to_text() + ")")
        failures = failures + 1

fn main() -> void:
    print("networking probe (#NET-001..010)")

    # --- TCP: bind a real listener on a kernel-assigned port ----------------
    val (tcp_handle, tcp_err) = native_tcp_bind("127.0.0.1:0")
    check_i64("tcp bind error code", tcp_err, 0)
    check_bool("tcp bind yields a live handle", tcp_handle > 0, true)
    if tcp_handle > 0:
        check_i64("tcp close succeeds", native_tcp_close(tcp_handle), 0)

    # A second bind must also succeed — proves the first close really released
    # the descriptor rather than the handle being a constant.
    val (tcp2, tcp2_err) = native_tcp_bind("127.0.0.1:0")
    check_i64("second tcp bind error code", tcp2_err, 0)
    check_bool("second tcp bind yields a live handle", tcp2 > 0, true)
    check_bool("second tcp handle is distinct from a closed one", tcp2 != 0, true)
    if tcp2 > 0:
        check_i64("second tcp close succeeds", native_tcp_close(tcp2), 0)

    # --- UDP ----------------------------------------------------------------
    val (udp_handle, udp_err) = native_udp_bind("127.0.0.1:0")
    check_i64("udp bind error code", udp_err, 0)
    check_bool("udp bind yields a live handle", udp_handle > 0, true)
    if udp_handle > 0:
        check_i64("udp close succeeds", native_udp_close(udp_handle), 0)

    # --- closing an invalid handle must not report success ------------------
    check_bool("closing an invalid tcp handle is not reported as success",
               native_tcp_close(-1) == 0, false)

    if failures == 0:
        print("PROBE VERDICT: PASS")
    else:
        print("PROBE VERDICT: FAIL (" + failures.to_text() + " checks wrong)")
